Read present_value.go's new one-time-expense leg (lines 63-89) plus the
worker's 3 new tests, then tried to break the design contract from first
principles rather than trusting the worker's own tests.

1. Timing off-by-one (engine charge month vs PV discount month):
   - Traced stepper.go: oneTimeExpenseThisMonth is set inside the
     `if monthInYear == 0` block (stepper.go:226-233), i.e. only at
     m % 12 == 0, so the engine charges a Year-N one-time expense exactly
     at month N*12 — matching present_value.go's `chargeMonth := year*12`.
   - Built a throwaway brute-force probe (not committed) that ran the real
     engine (engine.New().Run) with and without a Year=5/$50k expense,
     scanned every month of the actual ProjectionMonth.TotalExpenses for a
     diff, and asserted (a) exactly one month differs, (b) that month is
     year*12, (c) discounting the ENGINE's own charged delta by
     (1+monthlyRate)^(chargeMonth+1) matches the PVExpenses delta produced
     by PresentValue() to 1e-6 relative error.
     Result: PASS (had to fix my own probe first — DefaultWhatIfSettings()
     has PortfolioValue 0, so the engine depletes and stops after month 0;
     that was a bug in my probe, not in Z1, confirmed by re-running with
     PortfolioValue=50,000,000 and getting a clean single-month diff at
     exactly month 60).
   - Confirms the (m+1) ordinary-annuity convention is applied at the same
     month the engine actually charges — no off-by-one, no month drift
     relative to the living-expense/SS-optimizer/tax legs that share
     presentValueOfMonthlyStream.

2. Same-year double-counting: wrote a probe with two OneTimeExpense entries
   both Year=5 ($50k + $10k). PVExpenses diff vs. a no-expense fixture
   matched (60,000 * inflationFactor) discounted once at month 61 — not
   2x. The `oneTimeYears map[int]bool` dedup does what the comment claims;
   OneTimeExpensesForYear's internal per-year sum is called once per
   distinct year, not once per entry. PASS.

3. Boundary conditions:
   - Worker's own AtOrBeyondHorizon test covers Year==ProjectionYears and
     Year>ProjectionYears (both contribute 0) for a 10-year horizon —
     verified chargeMonth>=months skip is `>=` not `>`, so Year==10 with
     ProjectionYears=10 is correctly excluded (contributes 0), matching
     "Year >= ProjectionYears contributes 0" from the design contract.
   - Probed Year=0 separately: chargeMonth=0, and the (m+1) convention
     still discounts one month out (diff = 10000/(1+monthlyRate)^1 =
     9959.42, not 10000 flat) — consistent with every other leg in this
     file, not a special-cased exemption. PASS.

4. Coverage-ratio / other-consumer double-add: grepped every consumer of
   PVExpenses and PresentValueAnalysis (`grep -rn "PVExpenses"
   --include=*.go`) — only present_value.go writes it and only
   web/templates/components/whatif/present-value.html reads it, and that
   template renders the aggregate total (no separate one-time-expense
   line item that would need independent updating), so no split-
   classification risk on this surface.

5. Spending-phase double-count: LivingExpensesAtMonth (engine/loop_helpers.go)
   is a thin wrapper over the engine-local livingExpensesAtMonth
   (engine/expense.go), which computes only
   MonthlyLivingExpenses * spendingMultiplier * inflation — it does NOT
   fold in oneTimeExpenseThisMonth (that's a separate stepper-local
   variable added afterward into TotalExpenses, never into
   CurrentLivingExpenses/livingExpensesAtMonth). So the spending-phase
   branch of present_value.go (which discounts LivingExpensesAtMonth) and
   the new one-time-expense leg are additive, not overlapping.

6. BigTicketItems scope: confirmed present_value.go has no reference to
   BigTicketItems and the diff doesn't touch ApplyBigTicketItemsForYear —
   deliberately out of scope per the task, untouched.

7. Ran `go build ./...` and `go test ./internal/services/retirement/analysis/...
